.\"
.\" You can preview this file with:
.\"     $ man ./docs/iamb.1
.Dd Mar 24, 2024
.Dt IAMB 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm iamb
.Nd a terminal-based client for Matrix for the Vim addict
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl hV
.Op Fl P Ar profile
.Op Fl C Ar dir
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a client for the Matrix communication protocol.
It provides a terminal user interface with familiar Vim keybindings, and
includes support for multiple profiles, threads, spaces, notifications,
reactions, custom keybindings, and more.
.Pp
This manual page includes a quick rundown of the available commands in
.Nm .
For example usage and a full description of each one and its arguments, please
refer to the full documentation online.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl P , Fl Fl profile
The profile to start
.Nm
with.
If this flag is not specified,
then it defaults to using
.Sy default_profile
(see
.Xr iamb 5 ) .
.It Fl C , Fl Fl config-directory
Path to the directory the configuration file is located in.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Show the help text and quit.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Show the current
.Nm
version and quit.
.El

.Sh "GENERAL COMMANDS"
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy ":chats"
View a list of joined rooms and direct messages.
.It Sy ":commands" , Sy ":palette"
Open the command palette: a searchable list of what
.Nm
can do, showing which keys run it.
Type to filter, move through the list with the usual movement keys, and press
.Sy <Enter>
to run the selected entry.
.Pp
It lists every form of every
.Nm
command, including subcommands such as
.Sy ":room notify set"
and
.Sy ":verify confirm" ;
the keybindings
.Nm
defines; any keybindings from your own
.Xr iamb 5
configuration; and the slash commands.
An entry that still needs an argument is typed into the command bar and left for
you to finish rather than being run straight away.
Slash commands are listed for reference only, since they are typed at the start
of a message rather than run from here.
.Pp
Window and tab commands
.Pq Sy ":split" , Sy ":tabnext" , and so on
come from the underlying editor library along with the Vim motions and
operators.
Those cannot be enumerated, so they are not in the palette; see
.Sx "WINDOW COMMANDS"
and
.Sx "TAB COMMANDS"
below.
.It Sy ":dms"
View a list of direct messages.
.It Sy ":logout [user id]"
Log out of
.Nm .
.It Sy ":read"
Mark the focused room as read, or just the thread when viewing one.
In a list window
.Pq Sy ":rooms" , Sy ":dms" , Sy ":chats" , Sy ":unreads" , Sy ":threads" , Sy ":unreadsandthreads" , Sy ":unreadmentions"
this instead marks the currently selected entry as read.
Only the selected entry is affected; there is no way to mark a range of entries
read at once.
Bound to
.Sy <C-W>r .
.It Sy ":read all"
Mark every room and thread as read.
.It Sy ":rooms"
View a list of joined rooms.
.It Sy ":spaces"
View a list of joined spaces.
.It Sy ":search" Ar term
Search every room the homeserver can read for messages matching
.Ar term ,
and list what it finds, newest first.
The whole argument is the term, so
.Sy ":search deploy on friday"
searches for those three words.
.Pp
The list works like the quick switcher: type to narrow it down by message text,
sender, or room, move through it with the usual movement keys, and press
.Sy <Enter>
to go to the selected message where it was sent.
Narrowing the list does not search again; the search runs once, when the window
opens.
.Pp
An encrypted room is answered by the local message index instead of by the
homeserver, which cannot read it.
That index is off until you turn it on, and it holds only what it has indexed,
so the window title says how many rooms the search could not look in.
See
.Sx LOCAL INDEX
in
.Xr iamb 5 ,
and
.Sy ":reindex"
below.
.It Sy ":reindex"
Index the history of the focused room, so that
.Sy ":search"
can find messages that were sent before the local message index was turned on.
.Pp
Without history the index answers almost nothing worth asking, because what a
person searches for is usually something that was decided months ago.
.Pp
The forms are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy ":reindex"
Index the history of the focused room.
.It Sy ":reindex all"
Index the history of every encrypted room.
.It Sy ":reindex status"
Say how many rooms are done, how many messages went in, and which room is being
worked on now.
.It Sy ":reindex stop"
Stop indexing.
What was already indexed stays indexed.
.El
.Pp
Indexing runs in the background and is deliberately slow, so that the client
stays usable while it runs.
Expect hours for a large account.
You can keep using iamb, and you can quit: a later
.Sy ":reindex"
continues where this one stopped rather than starting again.
.Pp
A room whose keys are missing cannot be indexed, and those messages will not be
found by a search.
The end of a run says how many events that was.
If you have a key backup, run
.Sy ":keys recover"
and index again.
.It Sy ":switch" , Sy ":switcher"
Open the quick switcher: one fuzzy-matched list of everywhere there is to jump
to.
Type to narrow it down, move through the list with the usual movement keys, and
press
.Sy <Enter>
to go to the selected entry.
Bound to
.Sy <C-K> .
.Pp
It lists every room, direct message, and space you have joined, matched on name,
canonical alias, and room ID; and the windows that
.Nm
commands open, such as
.Sy ":rooms"
and
.Sy ":unreads" ,
matched on the command and on what it does.
Each entry says which it is: rooms are prefixed
.Sy # ,
direct messages
.Sy @ ,
spaces
.Sy + ,
and windows
.Sy \&: ,
and every entry is labelled in its own column.
Entries with unread activity are marked and shown in bold.
.Pp
How well an entry matches what you typed decides the order, so typing a room's
name in full puts that room first.
Entries that match equally well are ordered rooms before windows, unread before
quiet, and most recent first, which is also the order of the whole list before
you have typed anything.
.Pp
Selecting a room opens it exactly as selecting it in
.Sy ":rooms"
would.
.Sy <C-K>
is bound in normal and visual mode only, so it does not shadow the Vim digraph
key while you are typing a message.
.It Sy ":context"
Open the selected message where it lives, and select it there.
A thread reply is read in its thread, and the message that started a thread is
read in the main scrollback, so this goes to the one the message belongs to.
It reports an error when the message is already being read where it lives.
.It Sy ":snooze [duration]"
Defer the focused room, the thread being viewed, or the selected list entry, so
that it leaves
.Sy ":unreads" ,
.Sy ":threads" ,
.Sy ":unreadsandthreads"
and
.Sy ":unreadmentions"
until the time passes.
Nothing is marked read and no read receipt moves.
The duration is a number with
.Sy m , h , d
or
.Sy w ,
or the word
.Sy tomorrow .
With no argument the configured default is used.
When the time passes the entry returns at the top of the unread list, because it
sorts as the most recent thing there.
Desktop notifications for it are silenced while it is deferred, including direct
mentions.
Snoozing a room also defers the threads in that room, and an explicit snooze on
a thread always wins, whether it is sooner or later.
Wake times are stored in room account data, so they survive a restart and reach
your other clients.
.It Sy ":snoozed"
Open a list of what is deferred, soonest first, with the time each entry comes
back.
.Sy ":unsnooze"
cancels the snooze on the selected entry, which then returns to
.Sy ":unreadsandthreads" .
Selecting an entry opens the room or the thread without cancelling anything.
.It Sy ":unsnooze"
Cancel the snooze on the focused room, the thread being viewed, or the selected
list entry.
.It Sy ":threads"
View a list of threads you follow across all rooms.
A thread counts as followed if you started it, replied in it, or have a
thread-scoped read receipt for it.
.It Sy ":undoread"
Undo the most recent read, putting the read markers it moved back where they
were.
Repeating this walks back through earlier reads, most recent first.
A bulk read
.Pq Sy ":read all" , Sy ":unreads clear"
counts as one step, so a single
.Sy ":undoread"
reverses all of it.
Undoing a read of one thread affects only that thread, never the rest of its
room.
Bound to
.Sy <C-W>u .
.Pp
Only explicit reads are recorded.
If
.Sy read_receipt_manual
is off, merely viewing a room advances your read marker, and those advances are
not undoable.
.Pp
The undo history lives in memory and is not kept across restarts.
Restoring a read marker also cannot always be made to stick on the server:
Synapse ignores a read receipt that points at an event older than the one it
already has for you, so after a restart the room may read as read again.
.It Sy ":unreadmentions"
View a list of the unread rooms that mention you, and the unread direct
messages.
This is the narrow inbox: it holds only what is addressed to you, where
.Sy ":unreadsandthreads"
holds everything you have not read.
A room is here when the server or the client counts an unread message in it that
highlights you, so the room does not have to be one you are otherwise reading.
Entries are sorted the same way as in the other unread windows, and a deferred
entry is left out until it wakes.
.It Sy ":unreads"
View a list of unread rooms.
.It Sy ":unreads clear"
Mark all rooms as read.
.It Sy ":unreads threads" , Sy ":unreadsandthreads"
View a list of unread rooms and unread followed threads, intermixed.
.It Sy ":welcome"
View the startup Welcome window.
.It Sy ":forget"
Remove all left rooms from the internal database.
.El

.Sh "E2EE COMMANDS"
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy ":keys export [path] [passphrase]"
Export and encrypt keys to
.Pa path .
.It Sy ":keys import [path] [passphrase]"
Imports room keys from a file.
.It Sy ":keys recover " Ar recovery-key
Unlocks the server-side key backup with your recovery key, so that keys this
client is missing download by themselves.
Run this once for each session.
The whole rest of the line is the key, so the spaces in a grouped key are kept.
The key is used and not stored: what it unlocks goes to the encrypted store.
Without it a client can see that a backup exists and still cannot read it.
Import and decrypt keys from
.Pa path .
.It Sy ":verify"
View a list of ongoing E2EE verifications.
.It Sy ":verify accept [key]"
Accept a verification request.
.It Sy ":verify cancel [key]"
Cancel an in-progress verification.
.It Sy ":verify confirm [key]"
Confirm an in-progress verification.
.It Sy ":verify mismatch [key]"
Reject an in-progress verification due to mismatched Emoji.
.It Sy ":verify request [user id]"
Request a new verification with the specified user.
.El

.Sh "MESSAGE COMMANDS"
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy ":download [path]"
Download an attachment from the selected message and save it to the optional path.
.It Sy ":open [path]"
Download and then open an attachment, or open a link in a message.
.It Sy ":edit"
Edit the selected message.
.It Sy ":editor"
Open an external
.Ev $EDITOR
to compose a message.
.It Sy ":react [shortcode]"
React to the selected message with an Emoji.
.It Sy ":unreact [shortcode]"
Remove your reaction from the selected message.
When no arguments are given, remove all of your reactions from the message.
.It Sy ":pipe <command>"
Send the selected messages to a shell command on its standard input.
With a visual selection this sends every selected message, and otherwise it sends
the one message under the cursor.
The messages are sent as plain text, in the same form that
.Sy y
puts in a register.
The command is run by the shell named by
.Ev $SHELL ,
so a pipeline or a redirection in it works, as in
.Dl :pipe cat > /tmp/thread.txt
.Pp
The command runs in the background.
What it prints is shown on the status line, as is any failure to run it.
.It Sy ":redact [reason]"
Redact the selected message with the optional reason.
.It Sy ":reply"
Reply to the selected message.
.It Sy ":cancel"
Cancel the currently drafted message including replies.
.It Sy ":replied"
Go to the message the current message replied to.
.It Sy ":upload [path]"
Upload an attachment and send it to the currently selected room.
With no path, the image currently in the system clipboard is uploaded instead,
as a PNG named after the time it was sent.
Anything typed in the message bar is sent with the image as its caption, in the
same message, rather than as a separate message.
If the clipboard holds no image, nothing is sent and an error is shown.
.El

.Sh "ROOM COMMANDS"
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy ":create [arguments]"
Create a new room. Arguments can be
.Dq ++alias=[alias] ,
.Dq ++public ,
.Dq ++space ,
and
.Dq ++encrypted .
.It Sy ":invite accept"
Accept an invitation to the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":invite reject"
Reject an invitation to the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":invite send [user]"
Send an invitation to a user to join the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":join [room]"
Join a room or open it if you are already joined.
.It Sy ":leave"
Leave the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":members"
View a list of members of the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":room name set [name]"
Set the name of the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":room name unset"
Unset the name of the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":room dm set"
Mark the currently focused room as a direct message.
.It Sy ":room dm unset"
Mark the currently focused room as a normal room.
.It Sy ":room unread set"
Mark the currently focused room as unread.
.It Sy ":room unread unset"
Mark the currently focused room as read.
.It Sy ":room unread clear"
Alias for
.Sy :room unread unset .
.It Sy ":room notify set [level]"
Set a notification level for the currently focused room.
Valid levels are
.Dq mute ,
.Dq mentions ,
.Dq keywords ,
and
.Dq all .
Note that
.Dq mentions
and
.Dq keywords
are aliases for the same behaviour.
.It Sy ":room notify unset"
Unset any room-level notification configuration.
.It Sy ":room notify show"
Show the current room-level notification configuration.
If the room is using the account-level default, then this will print
.Dq default .
.It Sy ":room tag set [tag]"
Add a tag to the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":room tag unset [tag]"
Remove a tag from the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":room topic set [topic]"
Set the topic of the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":room topic unset"
Unset the topic of the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":room topic show"
Show the topic of the currently focused room.
.It Sy ":room alias set [alias]"
Create and point the given alias to the room.
.It Sy ":room alias unset [alias]"
Delete the provided alias from the room's alternative alias list.
.It Sy ":room alias show"
Show alternative aliases to the room, if any are set.
.It Sy ":room id show"
Show the Matrix identifier for the room.
.It Sy ":room canon set [alias]"
Set the room's canonical alias to the one provided, and make the previous one an alternative alias.
.It Sy ":room canon unset [alias]"
Delete the room's canonical alias.
.It Sy ":room canon show"
Show the room's canonical alias, if any is set.
.It Sy ":room ban [user] [reason]"
Ban a user from this room with an optional reason.
.It Sy ":room unban [user] [reason]"
Unban a user from this room with an optional reason.
.It Sy ":room kick [user] [reason]"
Kick a user from this room with an optional reason.
.El

.Sh "SPACE COMMANDS"
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy ":space child set [room_id] [arguments]"
Add a room to the currently focused space.
.Dq ++suggested
marks the room as a suggested child.
.Dq ++order=[string]
specifies a string by which children are lexicographically ordered.
.It Sy ":space child remove"
Remove the selected room from the currently focused space.
.El

.Sh "WINDOW COMMANDS"
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy ":horizontal [cmd]"
Change the behaviour of the given command to be horizontal.
.It Sy ":leftabove [cmd]"
Change the behaviour of the given command to open before the current window.
.It Sy ":only" , Sy ":on"
Quit all but one window in the current tab.
.It Sy ":quit" , Sy ":q"
Quit a window.
.It Sy ":quitall" , Sy ":qa"
Quit all windows in the current tab.
.It Sy ":resize"
Resize a window.
.It Sy ":rightbelow [cmd]"
Change the behaviour of the given command to open after the current window.
.It Sy ":split" , Sy ":sp"
Horizontally split a window.
.It Sy ":vertical [cmd]"
Change the layout of the following command to be vertical.
.It Sy ":vsplit" , Sy ":vsp"
Vertically split a window.
.El

.Sh "TAB COMMANDS"
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy ":tab [cmd]"
Run a command that opens a window in a new tab.
.It Sy ":tabclose" , Sy ":tabc"
Close a tab.
.It Sy ":tabedit [room]" , Sy ":tabe"
Open a room in a new tab.
.It Sy ":tabrewind" , Sy ":tabr"
Go to the first tab.
.It Sy ":tablast" , Sy ":tabl"
Go to the last tab.
.It Sy ":tabnext" , Sy ":tabn"
Go to the next tab.
.It Sy ":tabonly" , Sy ":tabo"
Close all but one tab.
.It Sy ":tabprevious" , Sy ":tabp"
Go to the preview tab.
.El

.Sh "SLASH COMMANDS"
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy "/markdown" , Sy "/md"
Interpret the message body as Markdown markup.
This is the default behaviour.
.It Sy "/html" , Sy "/h"
Send the message body as literal HTML.
.It Sy "/plaintext" , Sy "/plain" , Sy "/p"
Do not interpret any markup in the message body and send it as it is.
.It Sy "/me"
Send an emote message.
.It Sy "/confetti"
Produces no effect in
.Nm ,
but will display confetti in Matrix clients that support doing so.
.It Sy "/fireworks"
Produces no effect in
.Nm ,
but will display fireworks in Matrix clients that support doing so.
.It Sy "/hearts"
Produces no effect in
.Nm ,
but will display floating hearts in Matrix clients that support doing so.
.It Sy "/rainfall"
Produces no effect in
.Nm ,
but will display rainfall in Matrix clients that support doing so.
.It Sy "/snowfall"
Produces no effect in
.Nm ,
but will display snowfall in Matrix clients that support doing so.
.It Sy "/spaceinvaders"
Produces no effect in
.Nm ,
but will display aliens from Space Invaders in Matrix clients that support doing so.
.El

.Sh "MENTIONS"
While composing a message, typing
.Sy @
brings up a list of the room's members straight away, and typing more
characters narrows it down.
Both the localpart of the Matrix user ID and the display name are matched,
and the match is fuzzy: the characters you type only have to appear in order,
so
.Sy @gh
will find
.Dq "Grace Hopper" .
Press
.Sy <Tab>
or
.Sy <C-N>
to take somebody from the list, or
.Sy <C-P>
to walk it backwards.
The list is ordered with the closest match at the top, and that entry is
highlighted from the moment the list appears, so it is always clear who
.Sy <Tab>
is about to pick.
Finishing a name and pressing
.Sy <Tab>
takes the person you were typing.
.Pp
.Sy <Tab>
only takes a completion while a list is showing.
The rest of the time it types a tab, as it always has.
.Pp
The list only appears when the
.Sy @
starts a word, that is, at the beginning of the message or after a space.
An
.Sy @
in the middle of a word belongs to that word, so typing an email address does
not bring up the list.
Type a space or press
.Sy <Esc>
to dismiss the list without picking anybody; the
.Sy @
you typed stays in the message as ordinary text.
.Pp
Only members who are in the room or have been invited to it are offered.
.Pp
Selecting a member inserts a Markdown link to their
.Lk https://matrix.to/ matrix.to
permalink, such as
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[Grace Hopper](https://matrix.to/#/@grace:example.com)
.Ed
.Pp
The link is ordinary editable text, so it can be reworded or removed like
anything else in the message bar.
When the message is sent, it becomes a pill: the formatted body carries an
anchor pointing at the user, the plain-text fallback carries just the name,
and the mentioned users are listed in the
.Sy m.mentions
field of the event so that other clients notify them.
.Pp
Members who share a display name with somebody else in the room are labelled
with their user ID as well, and members who have not set a display name are
labelled with their user ID.
The permalink is always built from the user ID, so a mention resolves to the
right person even when two people go by the same name.
.Pp
Mentions are only turned into pills for messages that are interpreted as
markup, which is the default and
.Sy /me .
.Sy /plaintext
and
.Sy /html
send what you typed as you typed it.

.Sh "EMOJI SHORTCODES"
While composing a message, typing
.Sy \&:
brings up a list of Emoji straight away, and typing more characters narrows it
down.
The matching works exactly as it does for mentions: it is fuzzy, so the
characters you type only have to appear in order, and the closest match is at
the top of the list and highlighted.
.Sy <Tab>
and
.Sy <C-N>
take an entry, and
.Sy <C-P>
walks the list backwards.
.Pp
Both the shortcodes of an Emoji and its written-out name are matched, so
.Sy :polar bear
finds
.Sy :polar_bear:
just as
.Sy :plrbear
does.
.Pp
As with mentions, the list only appears when the
.Sy \&:
starts a word.
A
.Sy \&:
in the middle of a word belongs to that word, so times such as
.Sy 10:30
and URLs such as
.Lk https://iamb.chat
do not bring one up.
Type a space or press
.Sy <Esc>
to dismiss the list without picking anything.
.Pp
Taking an entry inserts the shortcode, such as
.Sy :polar_bear: ,
rather than the Emoji character itself, so that the list you are searching
stays readable and so that the text remains editable afterwards.
Messages interpreted as markup, which is the default, render shortcodes as
their Emoji when they are sent.
.Pp
The argument to
.Sy :react
and
.Sy :unreact
completes the same way, with or without a leading
.Sy \&: ,
and inserts the bare shortcode that those commands expect.

.Sh "ENVIRONMENT"
The behaviour of
.Nm
is affected by the following environment variables.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy "RUST_LOG"
Overwrites the value set by the
.Sy log_level
config value.
The syntax is defined at
.Lk https://docs.rs/tracing-subscriber/0.3.16/tracing_subscriber/filter/struct.EnvFilter.html

.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1: Starting with a specific profile
To start with a profile named
.Sy personal
instead of the
.Sy default_profile
value:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ iamb -P personal
.Ed
.Ss Example 2: Using an alternate configuration directory
By default,
.Nm
will use the XDG directories, but you may sometimes want to store
your configuration elsewhere.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ iamb -C ~/src/iamb-dev/dev-config/
.Ed
.Sh "REPORTING BUGS"
Please report bugs in
.Nm
or its manual pages at
.Lk https://github.com/ulyssa/iamb/issues
.Sh "SEE ALSO"
.Xr iamb 5
.Pp
Extended documentation is available online at
.Lk https://iamb.chat
